          ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\left(x+1\\right)}{{e}^{y}}.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2\\left(x+1\\right)}{{e}^{y}} \\\\ {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\left(x+1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\left(x+1\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int 2\\left(x+1\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2\\left(x+1\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{y} & ={\\left(x+1\\right)}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n{e}^{y}={\\left(x+1\\right)}^{2}+C.\n$$",
